Free a state's primops with the state.

EvalState::addPrimOp allocates each builtin with `new PrimOp` and
stores the pointer in a Value. A Value lives in collector memory, and
the collector runs no destructor, so the PrimOp - its name, args,
doc string and impl - is never freed. `nix` makes one state per
process and never sees it. An embedder that makes and drops states
leaks every builtin each time: 84.5 KiB of malloc memory per state,
measured with mallinfo2 and valgrind (huggorm#162). Over 99% of the
loss is this one stack.

The state now keeps its primops in a std::list and frees them in its
destructor. `ownPrimOp` is public, so an embedder that makes a primop
value of its own gives the state that one too.

--- a/src/libexpr/eval.cc
+++ b/src/libexpr/eval.cc
@@ -367,6 +367,12 @@
 
 EvalState::~EvalState() {}
 
+PrimOp * EvalState::ownPrimOp(PrimOp && primOp)
+{
+    std::lock_guard lock(ownedPrimOpsMutex);
+    return &ownedPrimOps.emplace_back(std::move(primOp));
+}
